## Manager Onboarding Playbook

**For Hiring Managers**

**Effective Date:** January 1, 2025  
**Version:** 1.0

---

## 1. Overview: Your Role in Onboarding

As a hiring manager, you are the primary driver of new hire success during the first 90 days. While HR handles administrative onboarding, and the company provides resources and support, you are accountable for integrating the new hire into your team, ensuring they understand their role and priorities, and setting them up to contribute meaningfully and quickly.

This playbook outlines your responsibilities before the new hire arrives, during the first week, the first 30 days, and the probation period.

---

## 2. Pre-Joining: Preparation (1–2 weeks before start date)

### 2.1 Hardware and Software Setup

By 2 weeks before the new hire's start date, submit a hardware and software request to IT:

**What to Request:**
- Laptop (specify if Apple or Windows and any required development tools)
- Monitor, keyboard, and mouse (if onsite)
- Phone (if applicable to role)
- VPN access and security certificates
- SaaS tool licenses or access (project management, analytics, CRM, etc.)
- Office equipment: desk, chair, access card

**Shipping for Remote Hires:**
- If the new hire is fully remote, arrange shipping of equipment to their address. Provide delivery tracking to them.
- For hybrid remote employees, arrange office pickup or shipping as appropriate.

**Timeline:**
- Request by: 2 weeks before start date
- Expected delivery: At least 1 week before start date
- If equipment won't arrive on time, escalate to IT lead and your VP immediately

### 2.2 Pre-Joining Communication

**Send a Warm Welcome Email (1 week before start date)**

Include:
- Your name, contact info, and availability
- Team description and what the team does
- Start time, location (office address or video link), and any special instructions
- Dress code for first day
- Your manager's office location or remote setup
- Parking information (if applicable)
- Who they'll meet on Day 1
- Excitement about their arrival!

Example:
> "Hi [Name], we're thrilled to have you joining BrightPath on [Date]! You'll be working with our Product team to develop analytics dashboards for our retail customers. Your first day is [Date] at 9:30 a.m. in our Bengaluru office (Building X, 3rd floor). I'll meet you at the reception desk. Looking forward to introducing you to the team!"

**Confirm Receipt:**
- Follow up if they don't acknowledge receipt
- Clarify any questions (remote setup, commute, what to bring, etc.)

### 2.3 Team Preparation

**Announce the New Hire to Your Team (1 week before start date)**

Share:
- New hire's name, role, and background (LinkedIn profile if comfortable)
- Start date
- How their role impacts the team
- Assign a buddy (an experienced peer, ideally with good communication and mentoring skills)

**Buddy Preparation:**
- Meet the buddy before the new hire arrives
- Clarify their role: Answer informal questions, help navigate office/systems, provide peer support
- Provide the buddy with:
  - The new hire's contact information and background
  - Office tour points and access information
  - Common questions and resources (where to get coffee, lunch, HR portal, shared drive)
  - Your expectations for buddy support (1–2 hours per day in the first week)

**Team Meeting Preparation:**
- Alert team members that the new hire will attend their first team meeting
- Prepare a 2-minute intro: What the new hire will be working on, how they fit, what everyone can do to support them

### 2.4 First 30-Day Plan

Create a draft 30-day onboarding plan in collaboration with the new hire before they start (if possible) or during the first few days:

**Template (30-Day Plan):**
```
Week 1: Learning & Settling In
- Day 1: Setup, compliance training, meet team
- Week 1: Read handbook, watch product demo, shadow 1 call
- Goal: Understand company, role, and team

Week 2–3: Foundations
- Learn core processes and SOPs relevant to role
- Complete role-specific training
- Clarify 2–3 priority projects
- Goal: Confident in basics

Week 4: First Deliverable
- Take on 1 small task (2–3 days work)
- Execute end-to-end
- Get feedback
- Goal: Demonstrate capability
```

Share this plan with HR and the new hire before start date.

### 2.5 Logistics Coordination with HR

Confirm with HR:
- Offer letter and employment documents signed
- Welcome email sent by HR
- Compliance training modules assigned
- Onboarding portal access ready
- Benefits enrollment materials prepared
- Probation process documented (30-day, 60-day, 90-day check-ins)

