## 9. Frequently Asked Questions for Managers

**Q: How involved should I be in the new hire's day-to-day work?**  
A: Week 1: Very involved (checking in daily). Weeks 2–4: Available daily but giving space. Weeks 5–8: Check in every 2–3 days. Weeks 9–13: Weekly 1:1s and less frequent check-ins. Goal: Increase their independence each week.

**Q: What if my new hire is struggling and I'm not sure if they'll make it?**  
A: Flag it with HR by Day 15–20. Don't wait until Day 90. Early intervention allows for support plans, training, or alternative arrangements. There's no shame in needing more support; it's actually good management.

**Q: Should I assign them big projects in the first 30 days?**  
A: No. Stick with smaller tasks (2–3 days). Big projects can wait until they're confident in basics. You want them to succeed and build confidence.

**Q: How do I balance being supportive without being overbearing?**  
A: Be consistently available and interested, but let them drive. Ask questions instead of giving answers. Check in, but don't micromanage. Show confidence in them even when they're learning.

**Q: What if they ask to work full-time from home when we're hybrid?**  
A: In the first 90 days, I'd encourage office days. They need to build relationships and observe how things work in person. After probation, if it's appropriate for their role, they can request long-term remote work with VP approval.

**Q: How do I document concerns without being negative?**  
A: Be factual and objective. Example of good documentation: "New hire took 3 days to complete a task I estimated at 1 day and made 5 typos in deliverables. Asked to set 1:1 check-ins to provide more support." This is neutral and specific.

